# =================================================================================
# How to read this file
# =================================================================================
# `cp .env.example .env` gives a working local development setup. Every value that
# differs in production is named in the comment above it, so nothing is defined twice:
# the file used to set DATABASE_URL, AUTH_SECRET, NEXTAUTH_SECRET and NEXTAUTH_URL in
# a "COOLIFY" block at the top and again below, and dotenv keeps the LAST occurrence —
# so filling in the top block and deploying silently ran on the development values
# (F-734). One variable, one definition.
#
# Every variable is labelled:
#   Runtime      — read while serving. Restart the process to apply. Never a rebuild.
#   Build-time   — inlined by `next build`. Needs a rebuild, and must be passed as a
#                  docker-compose.yml `app.build.args` entry, not just `environment:`.
#   Admin-managed — also editable in /admin/config, where a saved value WINS over this
#                  file. Set it here only as the fallback an untouched deployment reads.
#
# Coolify: paste the names you need into the service's environment. Anything absent from
# docker-compose.yml's `environment:` block never reaches the container.

# =================================================================================
# REQUIRED
# =================================================================================
# Runtime. Coolify/compose: the app reaches Postgres by service hostname —
#   postgresql://navroop:POSTGRES_PASSWORD@postgres:5432/navroop
# The value below points at docker-compose.dev.yml (`pnpm db:up`) on host 5433.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://openlovable:openlovable@127.0.0.1:5433/openlovable
# Runtime. Signs sessions and derives the key that encrypts stored secrets. Generate a
# long random string per environment; changing it makes saved secrets unreadable.
AUTH_SECRET=replace_with_a_long_random_string
# Runtime. Accepted alias for AUTH_SECRET; set either one.
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=
# Runtime. This installation's public origin. Production: https://your-coolify-fqdn
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Runtime. Encrypts Integration secrets. At least 32 bytes; the container refuses to
# start below that. Store it in a password manager off-server.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Runtime. Compose only — the app never reads these; docker-compose.yml passes them to
# the postgres service. POSTGRES_USER and POSTGRES_DB default to `navroop`.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change_me_long_random
# POSTGRES_USER=navroop
# POSTGRES_DB=navroop
# PORT=3000

# Build-time AND runtime. Inlined into the browser bundle by `next build`, and also read
# on the server. The GitHub App Manifest callback and static preview origins are built
# from it, and the app refuses to boot in production if it is unset, unparseable, or a
# different host from APP_URL. It must be passed as a build arg — a value supplied only
# under compose `environment:` reaches the Node process and never the browser (F-725).
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Runtime. Server-side origin for password-reset links and preview page assembly. Same
# host as NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL. Admin-managed as `app.url`.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Runtime. Accepted aliases for APP_URL, in case an existing install only set one of them.
# AUTH_URL=

# Runtime. Admin-managed (`app.cronSecret`). Required for the scheduled tasks to run at
# all: authorizeCron fails closed, so with this unset every cron request is 401 and the
# schedules simply never fire.
CRON_SECRET=replace_with_a_long_random_string

# =================================================================================
# DATABASES USED ONLY BY TESTS AND TOOLING
# =================================================================================
# Runtime. Must be a different database name than DATABASE_URL. Same Postgres is fine.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://openlovable:openlovable@127.0.0.1:5433/openlovable_test
# Runtime. Optional. Disposable DB for `prisma migrate diff --from-migrations`
# (verify schema-drift). Prisma may drop/recreate it. Must not be DATABASE_URL or
# TEST_DATABASE_URL. If unset, verify derives the same host/user as TEST_DATABASE_URL
# with database openlovable_shadow.
SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://openlovable:openlovable@127.0.0.1:5433/openlovable_shadow
# Runtime. Optional. Target for `scripts/restore-db.ts` so a restore drill never points
# at the live database.
# RESTORE_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://navroop:change_me@127.0.0.1:5433/navroop_restore
# Runtime. Required only when a pending migration drops a table or column, or changes a
# column type. The pre-migrate gate refuses the deploy otherwise.
# ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE_MIGRATION=true
# Runtime, for `pnpm run verify` only. Set to 1 to run the full step list (every Playwright
# project) without passing --full; anything else runs the short list.
# VERIFY_FULL=1
# Runtime, for `pnpm run verify` only. Set to 1 to run the pre-push light scope without
# passing --light: the fast fatal steps, with the slow half deferred to the CI verify run
# (docs/release.md, "verify order"). --full / VERIFY_FULL outranks it.
# VERIFY_LIGHT=1

# =================================================================================
# AI — optional here; /admin/config is the primary control
# =================================================================================
# Generation runs on DeepSeek and on nothing else (lib/ai/providers.ts). The key is
# normally set in Admin → Configuration, which overrides this file; the variable below is
# the fallback an untouched deployment reads (`ai.deepseek.apiKey`). With a key from
# neither source, planning and building fail with "DeepSeek is not configured".
#
# This block used to advertise "AI PROVIDERS - Need at least one" over AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY,
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, GROQ_API_KEY and an AI_PRIMARY_PROVIDER / AI_FALLBACK_* chain. Nothing
# read any of them, and DEEPSEEK_API_KEY — the one key generation actually needs — was absent,
# so an operator could fill this file in completely and still get "No AI provider is
# configured" on the first project (F-712).
#
# Runtime, admin-managed. Restart to apply.
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# Leave blank unless DeepSeek traffic is routed through a proxy or gateway.
# DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com
# Which DeepSeek model writes plans and code. All are reasoning models.
# deepseek-v4-flash (default, reasoning, faster), deepseek-v4-pro (reasoning, strongest),
# deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp (image input).
# AI_PRIMARY_MODEL=deepseek-v4-flash
# Runtime, admin-managed (`ai.deepseek.thinking`). Whether plan/build ask DeepSeek
# to reason. V4 thinks unless the request sends thinking: { type: "disabled" }.
# DEEPSEEK_THINKING=enabled
# How many generations may run at once. Raise it only if the DeepSeek quota allows.
# AI_PROVIDER_CONCURRENCY=2
# Runtime, admin-managed (`ai.fileContextTokenCap`). Token budget for the file context a
# follow-up prompt carries. Raising it makes every follow-up cost more; the built-in cap is
# 30000. Admin → Configuration overrides this and records who changed it.
# NAVROOP_FILE_CONTEXT_TOKEN_CAP=30000
# Per-million token prices are /admin/config only (`ai.cost.inputPerMillionUsd` and
# `ai.cost.outputPerMillionUsd`): they have no environment fallback, because a wrong
# number here would silently misprice every project. Blank means the built-in list price.

# Runtime. Image generation and alt text only (lib/assets/generate-image.ts): OpenAI is
# tried first, then Gemini, and with neither set image generation falls back to keyless
# stock photography. These are NOT generation providers — they cannot stand in for
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=

# =================================================================================
# AUTH + FIRST ADMIN
# =================================================================================
# Runtime. Required (local scrape). Admin-managed as `tooling.firecrawl.apiKey`.
# Get a key from https://firecrawl.dev — website imports fail without it.
FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=

# Runtime, admin-managed (`app.workspaceName`). The name this product uses when it creates
# things on your behalf: the GitHub App is "Navroop Deploy — <name>" and a new Sentry
# project takes this name. Applies on the next connect, no rebuild.
WORKSPACE_NAME=Navroop
# Build-time. The same name as the browser sees it — the dashboard heading and email
# templates. Inlined by `next build`, so changing it needs a rebuild and the matching
# docker-compose.yml build arg. It is also the accepted alias for WORKSPACE_NAME above,
# so an existing deployment that only ever set this one keeps working: set one, not both.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WORKSPACE_NAME=Navroop

# Seed exactly one ADMIN (prisma/seed.ts). SEED_ADMIN_* is preferred; ADMIN_* is also accepted.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@navroop.local
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=ChangeMeNow123
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@navroop.local
ADMIN_PASSWORD=ChangeMeNow123
# Optional. The demo member (member@navroop.local, committed password) is seeded only when
# NODE_ENV != production; a production operator must opt in explicitly. Runtime for the
# seed process only — do not set in Coolify.
# SEED_DEMO_MEMBER=1
# Optional. Runtime — restart to apply. Default on: the documented deployment fronts the app
# with Coolify/Traefik, so the login/forgot-password rate limiters key on the LAST
# x-forwarded-for hop (the address the proxy appended). Set to 0 when the Node process is
# exposed directly; the limiters then ignore proxy headers and key on email alone.
# TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=1

# Build-time. Local /login quick-login buttons. Ignored when NODE_ENV=production, which is
# why docker-compose.yml deliberately does not pass it as a build arg. `next dev` already
# enables the buttons; set this only if you need them in a non-production build.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DEV_QUICK_LOGIN=true

# Runtime. GitHub OAuth account-linking (not login), admin-managed under `github.oauth.*`.
# Register the callback URL on the GitHub OAuth App.
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GITHUB_OAUTH_CALLBACK_URL=http://localhost:3000/api/github/callback

# Publish integrations (GitHub App, Cloudflare, Coolify) are configured in /admin/integrations.
# Do not set GITHUB_APP_*, CLOUDFLARE_*, ROOT_DOMAIN, or COOLIFY_API_TOKEN.

# =================================================================================
# EMAIL
# =================================================================================
# Runtime, admin-managed (`email.resend.apiKey`, `email.from`). Transactional email —
# invitations and password resets. An empty RESEND_API_KEY logs the full email to the
# console instead of sending it, and reset links must be copied from the Team page.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=

# =================================================================================
# PREVIEW
# =================================================================================
# Runtime, admin-managed (`preview.host`). A hostname pointing at this same application,
# used only to serve generated previews so their scripts run on a different origin from
# the app. Filled automatically when a Cloudflare zone is connected; without either,
# previews cannot be opened in a new tab (the in-app preview keeps working).
# PREVIEW_STATIC_HOST=preview.navroop.example.com

# =================================================================================
# IMAGE STORAGE + PICTURES
# =================================================================================
# Runtime, admin-managed (`storage.*`).
# local (default): writes to STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR (or /public/uploads) and returns a
# relative URL. Set to `s3` for ElasticLake / any S3-compatible store (path-style); the
# bucket must allow public reads or images will not load in generated sites.
STORAGE_DRIVER=local
# STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR=
# The ELK_* names are the primary ones. S3_ENDPOINT, S3_REGION, S3_BUCKET,
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID and S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY are accepted aliases for the five below, and
# ELK_PUBLIC_URL is an accepted alias for S3_PUBLIC_URL — set one name per value, not both.
# ELK_ENDPOINT=https://s3.example.com
# ELK_REGION=us-east-1
# ELK_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# ELK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# ELK_BUCKET=navroop-assets
# S3_PUBLIC_URL=https://assets.example.com

# Runtime, admin-managed (`tooling.images.*`). Self-hosted Cloudflare Worker that
# generates the pictures a build asks for. It must accept POST / with a JSON body of
# {"prompt","model"} and answer with image bytes; the token is sent as a bearer header and
# the endpoint is not called at all without it. With no worker, generation falls back to
# OPENAI_API_KEY or GEMINI_API_KEY, then to keyless stock photography.
# IMAGE_WORKER_URL=https://images.example.workers.dev
# IMAGE_WORKER_TOKEN=
# Verified models: lucid-origin (default), flux-2-klein-4b, flux-2-klein-9b, flux-1-schnell.
# IMAGE_WORKER_MODEL=lucid-origin

# Runtime, admin-managed (`tooling.unsplash.*`). Stock photography for generated sites;
# placeholders are used without a key. The free-tier access key is enough.
UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=
# Reference only — nothing sends either of these anywhere. They exist so the credential
# set stays together and an operator can tell which Unsplash application to rotate.
# UNSPLASH_APPLICATION_ID=
# UNSPLASH_SECRET_KEY=

# =================================================================================
# BACKUPS
# =================================================================================
# Runtime, admin-managed (`backups.*`). Separate bucket from ELK_BUCKET — the app refuses
# to boot if they match. Write+list only is recommended.
# BACKUP_ENDPOINT=https://s3.example.com
# BACKUP_REGION=us-east-1
# BACKUP_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# BACKUP_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# BACKUP_BUCKET=navroop-backups
# Used instead of a bucket when set. Development only: a backup on the same disk as the
# database is not a backup.
# BACKUP_LOCAL_DIR=

# =================================================================================
# CRON (Coolify scheduled tasks — Bearer CRON_SECRET, defined above)
# =================================================================================
# POST /api/cron/check-domains (every 2 min)
# POST /api/cron/reap-jobs (every minute)
# POST /api/cron/thin-checkpoints (daily)
# POST /api/cron/purge-projects (daily)
# POST /api/cron/check-integrations (daily)
# POST /api/cron/cleanup-orphans (daily)
# POST /api/cron/backup-db (daily 02:00)
# POST /api/cron/verify-storage (weekly)
# POST /api/cron/observability-heartbeat (hourly)
# POST /api/cron/observability-quota (daily)
# POST /api/cron/check-uptime (every 10 min)
# POST /api/cron/check-certs (daily)
# POST /api/cron/system-checks-digest (daily)
# POST /api/cron/sweep-tmp (hourly)
# Runtime, admin-managed retention windows.
# CHECKPOINT_RETENTION_DAYS=7
# PURGE_DELETED_DAYS=30

# =================================================================================
# PERSISTENT VOLUME (cache + bootstrap only — reconstructible)
# =================================================================================
# Runtime. Coolify: mount a volume at /data. The image chowns it to uid 1001 (nextjs).
# Not in the DB backup. Safe to delete: next boot rebuilds from Postgres / object storage.
# DATA_DIR=/data
# OBSERVABILITY_CONFIG_PATH=/data/config/observability.json

# =================================================================================
# LOGGING
# =================================================================================
# Runtime. Restart to apply, no rebuild. Threshold for the structured logger
# (`lib/logger.ts`): debug | info | warn | error. Anything below it is dropped before the
# line is even built. Default is `info` when NODE_ENV=production and `debug` otherwise, so
# an existing deployment gets quieter only if it asks. Deliberately NOT admin-managed: the
# logger is synchronous and runs during module initialisation, where a database read is
# impossible and a per-instance cached value would be a setting that appears to apply and
# does not.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# =================================================================================
# RELEASE IDENTITY + ERROR TRACKING
# =================================================================================
# Runtime. Which build is running, for /api/health and the rollback comparison. Coolify
# sets GIT_SHA and DEPLOYED_AT on the compose service; SOURCE_COMMIT is the fallback name
# read when GIT_SHA is absent.
# GIT_SHA=
# SOURCE_COMMIT=
# DEPLOYED_AT=
# Runtime. Coolify application UUID for the main Navroop app (rollback and the Sentry
# restart action). Not a client site.
# COOLIFY_APP_UUID=

# Sentry is configured in /admin/integrations, not here. These are read once, on FIRST
# boot only: if no Sentry Integration exists yet, SENTRY_DSN (or NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN)
# creates a "Sentry (migrated)" integration and the variables are ignored from then on.
# Runtime.
# SENTRY_DSN=
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
# Without an auth token the migrated integration is "limited": errors are received, quota
# monitoring is not. ORG and PROJECT scope the quota queries.
# Runtime AND build-time: `next.config.ts` also uses ORG, PROJECT and AUTH_TOKEN to upload
# source maps, so a change to them for that purpose needs a rebuild, not a restart. All
# three unset means no source-map upload — the build succeeds without them.
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
# SENTRY_ORG=
# SENTRY_PROJECT=
# Build-time. Fallback DSN for statically prerendered pages, which render before the
# runtime config file on the volume exists. Live pages read a <meta> tag instead, so an
# admin DSN change applies on the next page load with no rebuild.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
